This product builds on the following work.

Kokoro-82M
  Text-to-speech model by hexgrad, Apache License 2.0.
  https://huggingface.co/hexgrad/Kokoro-82M
  speak downloads the ONNX export of the model and its voices from the kokoro-onnx project at
  first run; the weights are not part of this repository or of speak's binaries.

kokoro-onnx (https://github.com/thewh1teagle/kokoro-onnx), MIT License, Copyright (c) thewh1teagle
  speak's Kokoro backend follows kokoro-onnx 0.4.7's inference pipeline (phoneme batching,
  style selection, silence trimming), and src/vocab.rs is generated from its config.json.

misaki (https://github.com/hexgrad/misaki), Apache License 2.0, by hexgrad
  The espeak-ng to misaki phoneme conversion tables in src/g2p/espeak.rs are taken from
  misaki 0.9.4 (misaki/espeak.py).

misaki-rs (https://github.com/MicheleYin/misaki-rs), MIT License
  English grapheme-to-phoneme conversion, including misaki's lexicons, linked into speak.

ONNX Runtime (https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime), MIT License, Copyright (c) Microsoft
  Corporation. Linked statically into speak's binary (prebuilt by pyke.io for the `ort` crate);
  ONNX Runtime's own third-party notices apply to binaries that contain it.

librosa (https://github.com/librosa/librosa), ISC License
  src/trim.rs implements the silence trimming of librosa.effects.trim, as kokoro-onnx uses it.

espeak-ng (https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng), GNU GPL version 3 or later
  speak never links or loads espeak-ng. When available, it runs the separate `espeak-ng`
  program for words its English lexicons don't know and for non-English voices.
  scripts/build-espeak.sh builds espeak-ng from its unmodified release source for development.
